      "inputA_hex": "a0c2",
      "inputA_note": "A0 is a stray UTF-8 continuation byte as a lead byte -> decode fails -> raw BYTE reverse -> C2A0",
      "inputB_hex": "c2a0",
      "inputB_note": "valid UTF-8 for U+00A0 (NBSP), a single codepoint -> CODEPOINT reverse of one codepoint is the identity -> re-encodes to C2A0",
      "bothMapTo_hex": "c2a0",
      "verifiedBy": "node -e require(../cascade/cascade_lib.js).reverseBuf on both inputs, both equal c2a0"
    },
    "consequenceForThisRun": "The enumeration itself is exact and unaffected -- every one of the 6,229,504 chains is a concrete, independently computed operation sequence scored on its own merits, none skipped or deduplicated because of this. What is NOT exact is the analytic null model: the union-bound f(L) formula assumes a fixed-uniform byte distribution is preserved (in law) across every gate-scored position, including positions downstream of a reverse boundary. Because reverseBuf is non-injective, it is a many-to-one map on the space of intermediate buffers, so it does not obviously preserve a uniform distribution over that space the way an injective/bijective permutation would. We have NOT proven uniformity is preserved through the byte-reverse/codepoint-reverse branch selection, so the analytic P(max>=L) bound below is reported as an approximation for chains that include one or more reverse boundaries, not an exact bound. The empirical negative (546 random bytes, identical sweep, identical reverse operator) is the reliable check for this run, since it experiences the exact same non-injectivity as rev7 and needs no distributional assumption."
